  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How clever are octopuses?",
      "a": "Very, for an invertebrate. Octopuses learn by trial and error, open jars, find their way through mazes, use coconut shells as shelters and remember solutions. Their intelligence evolved separately from that of mammals and birds.",
      "asks": [
        "Are octopuses intelligent?",
        "Can octopuses solve puzzles?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do octopus arms think for themselves?",
      "a": "Partly. About two thirds of an octopus's nerve cells are in its arms, and each arm can sense and react on its own, even grabbing food, while the brain decides overall what to do.",
      "asks": [
        "Does an octopus have nine brains?",
        "Can an octopus arm move on its own?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can octopuses taste with their arms?",
      "a": "Yes. Their suckers have receptors that detect chemicals, so an octopus tastes whatever it touches, helping it find food hidden in cracks without seeing it.",
      "asks": [
        "Can octopus suckers taste?",
        "How do octopuses find food in the dark?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do octopuses escape from tanks?",
      "a": "An octopus has no bones, so it can squeeze through any gap bigger than its hard beak. In 2016 an octopus called Inky famously slipped out of his tank at New Zealand's National Aquarium and down a drainpipe to the sea.",
      "asks": [
        "Can an octopus squeeze through small holes?",
        "Did an octopus really escape an aquarium?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How long do octopuses live?",
      "a": "Most octopuses live only one or two years, and the giant Pacific octopus about three to five. Females usually die soon after their eggs hatch, so octopuses do all their learning in a short life.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an octopus's lifespan?",
        "Why do octopuses die so young?"
      ]
    }
  ],
